00:11:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair point, and I appreciate you bringing your own experience into the conversation—it makes this feel concrete and real. You’re absolutely right that for many people, many times, a prescription just *works* without any noticeable side effects. That’s not luck; it’s the triumph of exactly the kind of precise drug design we’ve been talking about. So if your reality is “no side effects ever happened to me,” it can definitely sound like the idea of imperfect selectivity is a theoretical problem that doesn’t pan out in the real world. Let me reframe a little, and I think you’ll see that your experience actually *proves* the design principle rather than contradicting the complexity. ### Your experience is the goal, not the exception When a drug is well-designed, it targets the disease mechanism so precisely and is dosed so carefully that the off-target effects stay below a threshold you can feel. In other words, **the “imperfect selectivity” is still there at the molecular level, but it’s so small or so well-buffered by your body that it never surfaces as a symptom.** That’s the magic. A few things that often make this possible, especially with the kinds of drugs you mentioned: - **Antibiotics** (say, amoxicillin): They target bacterial cell wall synthesis, a feature human cells don’t have. So at the *primary* effect level, they are incredibly selective. Many people take a course and feel completely fine. However, the antibiotic might still knock out some friendly gut bacteria, which could cause a slightly softer stool or mild stomach gurgles that you’d never attribute to the drug because you’re also getting over an infection that might mess with your digestion anyway. You didn’t notice it—so in your experience, perfect selectivity held up. But the drug did have a minor off-target “whisper” on your microbiome; it just never crossed the line into a side effect you’d label as such. - **Painkillers** (like ibuprofen): They block COX enzymes to reduce pain and inflammation. Amazingly effective. Many people pop them with zero issues. But ibuprofen also blocks COX-1 in the stomach lining, which normally helps protect it from acid. In a young, healthy person with a robust stomach, that tiny bit of reduced protection is completely invisible—you wouldn’t feel a thing. The selectivity is imperfect at the enzyme level (it hits both COX-1 and COX-2, though we later designed more selective COX-2 inhibitors), but your body’s resilience absorbs the impact perfectly. - **Individual variation**: Some people have genetic quirks, other medications, or underlying sensitivities that make the same off-target “nudge” suddenly become a raging headache, nausea, or worse. You may simply be one of the many who have an especially wide safety buffer, so the same mild off-target interactions that would cause a problem in someone else stay silent in you. ### The bottom line – it’s a matter of threshold, not absence Think of it like background noise. The drug is a perfect signal aimed at the disease, but it also sends a very faint hum in some other directions. If the hum is 10 decibels below what your body can hear, you’ll say “no side effects at all.” That’s exactly what we want. The scientific explanation doesn’t say you’re wrong; it says the hum exists, but good design and your own physiology keep it completely muted. So your actual experience and the whole “imperfect selectivity” concept don’t conflict. Your experience is the living proof that we can make that imperfection so vanishingly small that it’s practically irrelevant for you. I’m genuinely glad that’s been your journey—it’s what the whole pharmaceutical enterprise is striving for, and you’re a success story of that effort.
