00:12:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a completely valid point, and I won’t try to have it both ways. In everyday language, you're absolutely right: **a side effect that nobody feels or notices isn’t much of a side effect at all.** From a patient’s perspective, if the drug fixes the problem and you go about your day without a single new ache, rash, or weird feeling, then for all practical purposes it had no side effects. That's the gold standard, and it sounds like you've experienced that – which is excellent. The only difference I’m pointing to isn’t about your experience being wrong; it’s about the definitions doctors and scientists use behind the scenes. In medicine, “side effect” is defined much more broadly: **any unintended effect of a drug, whether the person feels it or not.** That can include tiny changes in blood pressure, a slight bump in a liver enzyme, or a subtle shift in gut bacteria – none of which you’d ever perceive. So scientifically, a drug can be imperfectly selective at the molecular level and still be perfect *in your life*. Those two facts aren’t contradictory; they’re just measured on different levels. Think of it like this: a car engine may have a microscopic imbalance that causes a vibration at 0.001 mm – an engineer would call that a “secondary effect” of the design. But if no driver can ever feel it, and it never causes wear, then as far as the driver is concerned, the engine is perfectly smooth. Both the engineer and the driver are correct; they’re just using different scales. You’re the driver, and you’re right: it’s a smooth ride. So I’m not trying to have it both ways; I’m simply distinguishing between the clinical textbook definition (which acknowledges that all drugs have some degree of biological “leakage”) and the real-world definition that actually matters to the person taking the pill. Your experience is the truest test for you, and I think it’s fantastic that modern medicines have delivered that outcome so consistently for you. That’s the whole point of the selectivity effort – to make the off-target effects so negligible they vanish from your reality.
