00:14:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. You’re making a purely logical point, and you want a straight, unhedged answer to match it. I’ll give you that. You defined “exact” as meaning the drug does nothing else. If a drug was truly designed to do exactly one thing and nothing else, then by definition there would be zero other effects. That’s a tautology—completely correct. So if a drug has any side effect, felt or unfelt, that proves the design was not exact enough. So here’s my straight answer: **in practice, absolutely exact targeting—zero interaction with anything else in the entire body—is physically impossible, not because design is sloppy, but because of how biological targets are built.** Here’s the one time I’ll lay out the “why” without it being a sneaky caveat: The lock‑and‑key idea is brilliant, but in biology, the “locks” (proteins) are not unique shapes crafted from scratch. They all evolved from a common set of folds and amino acids. The human body has over **20,000 proteins**, many with similar pockets because they all bind the same tiny molecules (ATP, NAD, etc.). The ATP-binding pocket of BCR-ABL, which imatinib targets, has close relatives in hundreds of other kinases. Even the most exquisitely designed key will have a tiny, non‑zero affinity for some of those similar locks. It’s a numbers game: you can optimize the key to bind 10,000 times more tightly to the target than to any other, but you cannot mathematically reduce the off‑target affinity to absolute zero across all possible locks at all times, because the shapes are too similar and the body is a vast sea of them. So **exact targeting, in the absolute sense you mean, is impossible not because we haven’t tried hard enough, but because nature didn’t give us a unique lock.** That’s the fundamental ceiling. No amount of better design can make a molecule that is inert to every protein except one when those proteins share structural motifs. It’s like trying to design a key that opens only your front door but physically cannot enter the keyway of any other lock in the world—possible only if every other lock is radically different. But biological locks are all variations on a few themes. So, to your core statement: **side effects don’t just “prove it wasn’t designed exactly enough” as if we could get there with more effort. They reflect the hard physical reality that getting to absolute zero is a physical impossibility, not a design shortfall.** The best we can achieve is side effects that are so rare, minor, or clinically invisible that they don’t matter—which is what you experienced. That’s not imperfection of the drug; it’s perfection of the drug *given the constraints of the system it operates in*. I’ve avoided any “both ways” phrasing. That’s the straight truth: absolute exactness is a physical impossibility in a human body; but **practical exactness—the kind you felt, where nothing bad happened—is absolutely achievable and routinely achieved**. The two are different tiers of reality.
